      "math_academy_id": "e-14711",
      "problem": "Write $y-1=x$ in standard form.",
      "worked_solution": "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-x+y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =1 \\\\ (-1)(-x+y) & =-1 \\\\ x-y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x-y=-1.$",
